The Use of == operator on floating point values checker finds instances of using the `==` operator on floating-point numbers.
Comparison between floating-point numbers may not always function correctly.
Instead of using the `==` operator, use Math.Abs to check whether the difference between two numbers falls within the error tolerance. For example, you can compare as follows: const double EPSILON = 0.001; Console.WriteLine(Math.Abs((0.1 + 0.2) - 0.3) < EPSILON);

SQL injection is an attack that exploits weaknesses in website SQL queries that are completed with user input values, and tampering with the input values to assemble or execute abnormal SQL queries. By causing SQL statements to be executed that the developer did not think of, it is possible to manipulate the database abnormally.

The malicious SQL query allows an attacker to view or manipulate DBMS or data. Make sure to filter out user inputs. Special characters, such as ```, `;`, `--`, `#`, `/* */` might cause the SQL injection. Do not use them in user inputs.

The followings are examples to filter out user inputs for each language.

Java:

```
String userInput = ...
Connection connection = getConnection();

String sql = ... 
try (PreparedStatement statement = connection.prepareStatement(sql)) {
	statement.setString(1, userInput); 
	ResultSet resultSet = statement.executeQuery();
	...
	while (resultSet.next()) {
		...
	}
}
```

